  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$",
  "worked_solution": "- Statement I and II are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
